rewritten_text:
I had no fear on her account. She was what people call a capable woman, and could handle her own affairs without me. To tell the truth, she probably always saw me as an idle boy, a useless extra, fit only for daydreams, and was rather relieved to see me go. It was a dark night when I said goodbye to her, and, taking along the three creditors who had plagued me, we carried the balloon, with its car and gear, by a roundabout route to the station where the rest of my equipment was stored. There we found everything untouched, and I set to work at once.

“It was the first of April. The night, as I said, was dark; no stars were visible; and a drizzling rain, falling now and then, made us miserable. My main worry was the balloon, which, despite the varnish meant to protect it, was getting heavy with moisture; the powder was at risk too. I therefore kept my three duns hard at work, pounding down ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. They never stopped pestering me with questions about what I meant to do with all this gear, and complained bitterly about the exhausting labor I forced on them. They said they could not see what good would come from being soaked to the skin just to take part in such awful rites. I grew uneasy and worked with all my strength, for I truly believe the fools thought I had made a pact with the devil, that what I was doing was no better than outright wickedness. I feared they would abandon me. I managed, however, to calm them with promises that I would settle every debt in full as soon as I finished the business at hand. They took these promises in their own way, no doubt thinking I would soon get plenty of ready cash, and that as long as I paid what I owed, plus a little extra for their help, they cared very little about what happened to my soul or my body.

“In about four and a half hours I found the balloon full enough. I fastened the car, loaded all my instruments into it, and did not forget the condensing apparatus, a generous supply of water, and plenty of provisions, including pemmican, which packs a lot of nutrition into little space."}

Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not annihilated by specters. The perpetrators were corporeal and made a tangible escape. By what means? Happily, only one line of reasoning lies open to us, and that line must bring us to a firm conclusion. Let us inspect, one by one, the possible outlets. It is evident that the killers were either in the chamber where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was discovered, or else in the adjoining room, when the rescuers climbed the stairs. We must search for exits only from these two quarters. The authorities stripped the floors, ceilings, and the brickwork of the walls in every direction. No hidden openings could have slipped their scrutiny. Still, unwilling to accept merely their eyesight, I made my own inspection. There were no secret passages. Both doors leading from the rooms into the corridor were fastened on the inside, and the keys lay within. Let us examine the chimneys. Although these were of common breadth for approximately eight or ten feet above the hearths, they would not admit, throughout their height, the body of even a large cat. Since escape by the paths already reviewed is absolutely impossible, we are forced back upon the windows. Through the front chamber’s windows, no one could have fled without being seen by the crowd in the street. The criminals must, therefore, have gone out by those of the rear room. Having been driven to this conclusion so unequivocally, we have no right, as reasoners, to dismiss it because it seems impossible. All that remains is to demonstrate that these seeming impossibilities are not such in truth.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One of them is unobstructed by furniture and wholly in view. The lower part of the other is concealed by the headboard of the ponderous bedstead thrust close against it. The first was discovered securely fastened inside. It resisted every bit of strength brought to bear by those attempting to lift it. A wide gimlet-hole was bored in the frame at the left, and an unusually stout nail was fitted in it nearly up to the head. When the second window was inspected, an identical nail was fitted in the same fashion; and a vigorous attempt to raise this sash failed likewise. The police were completely convinced that no one escaped by these avenues. Therefore, they thought it needless to remove the nails and open the windows.

“My own inspection went into finer detail, for precisely the reason already stated—because it was here, I knew, that all seeming impossibilities must be shown to be nothing of the sort.”}

While passing my hand through the valve to offer a cup of water to the old cat, the sleeve of my shirt snagged in the loop that held the basket, and in that instant I loosened it from its fastenings. If the whole affair had evaporated into thin air it could scarcely have vanished from my sight with more suddenness. Truly, not a tenth of a second could have elapsed between the basket’s release and its utter disappearance with everything inside. My well wishes pursued it earthward, yet I felt no expectation that either the cat or her kittens would survive to recount their misfortune.

“At six o’clock I saw a great stretch of the eastern horizon buried in thick shadow, which swept forward at high speed until, at five minutes to seven, the entire landscape lay wrapped in nighttime darkness. The balloon, however, remained bathed in the sun’s parting glow for a long while after; and though I had foreseen this, it delighted me beyond measure. Clearly, come morning, I should greet the rising luminary hours before the citizens of Rotterdam, despite their more easterly position, and thus, with each day and each increase in altitude, I would enjoy the sun’s light for longer spans. I now resolved to keep a journal of the voyage, counting the days in uninterrupted stretches of twenty-four hours, without regard to the intervals of night.

“At ten o’clock, feeling drowsy, I resolved to lie down for the remainder of the night; but a difficulty arose, obvious enough yet unnoticed by me until that moment. If I slept as planned, how was the air within the chamber to be renewed meanwhile? It would be impossible to breathe it for more than an hour at most, or, even stretching the limit to an hour and a quarter, the consequences might prove disastrous. This dilemma troubled me greatly; and it seems scarcely credible that, after all the perils already faced, I should contemplate it so seriously as to surrender any hope of achieving my final objective and to decide I must descend. Yet this wavering lasted only an instant.”}
